# OUTSIDE NEGENTROPY: the keyless-human, dyad-edge identity model against the field

## 0. Method, and the limit on this report's reach

`WebSearch` was **unavailable for this entire session** — the 200-call budget was spent before I was invoked, and every call failed. All six of us (myself + five parallel research spirits) worked through: direct `WebFetch` on guessed/known URLs, the Hacker News Algolia API, the GitHub search API via `gh`, OpenAlex, Crossref, PubMed, and the Wikipedia API. Google/Bing/DuckDuckGo/Mojeek/Startpage/searx are all captcha-walled or return junk. **Reddit is unreachable at the tool level** — likely the richest source of individual "I lost everything" narratives, entirely absent here.

Consequence: coverage is **excellent** where primary-source URLs are guessable (specs, vendor docs, GitHub, HN) and **thin** where discovery was needed (GSMA reports, ACM DL full text, Guifi.net governance, Swedish BankID fraud stats, EUDI pilot findings). I mark unreached items as **NOT REACHED** rather than filling them from memory. §11 lists them.

---

## 1. Who else has said "the human holds no key" — and what they measured

### 1.1 The purest deployed instance, and the industry's retreat from it

**Original FIDO2/WebAuthn (2018)** is the closest thing to ① at planetary scale: a non-exportable credential per authenticator per relying party. No portable identity object exists. Losing the device loses that relationship, by design. *Confidence: high.*

The industry then **walked away from it**. The Apple/Google/Microsoft joint announcement (May 2022, fidoalliance.org) introduced synced passkeys purely as a usability fix — and **never mentions the security tradeoff at all**. That silence is itself a finding: the field's flagship communication never named the thing your model is built around.

The clearest institutional admission that the tradeoff is real comes from **Microsoft's own current Entra documentation** (learn.microsoft.com), which gates the two models apart:
- *"Device-bound passkeys: The private key is created and stored on a single physical device and never leaves it."*
- *"Synced passkeys... do not support attestation."* — **"If attestation is enabled, only device-bound passkeys are allowed; synced passkeys are excluded."**
- Recommendation: device-bound for regulated industries and privileged users; synced for everyone else.

**Measured, and it cuts against the dyad on UX grounds** — Microsoft's published synced-passkey numbers across "hundreds of millions" of accounts: **99% successful registration; 3s vs 69s sign-in (14× faster than password+MFA); 95% vs 30% sign-in success rate** vs legacy auth. *Confidence: high that these are Microsoft's own published figures; methodology unverified.*

### 1.2 The device-binding patch that the spec's own reviewers said cannot work

This is the sharpest technical finding in the whole sweep. Traced through `gh api repos/w3c/webauthn`:

- **Adam Langley (Google) opened issue #1658, Aug 2021**, proposing `devicePubKey` explicitly "to potentially address some of the challenges of introducing syncable credentials." Mechanism: when a synced credential first appears on a new device, the authenticator also mints a local non-exportable device-bound key and attests it, so an RP can detect novelty.
- It survived PRs #1663, #1695, #1753, #1787, #1807, #1812, #1817, then in **PR #1957 (Sept 2023) was renamed `devicePubKey` → `supplementalPubKeys`**. It was **not abandoned** — it was generalized.
- A reviewer's live objection, verbatim: *"I can't really see what's the benefit of the new device key. It is authorized on first use by a signature chain from an already-registered, synced key... So if the desire is to only rely on device-bound keys, it seems like that promise is already broken by the time the new device-bound key is registered."*

**A copied key cannot be patched back into a device-bound one after the fact.** The W3C's own reviewers said so, in the spec repo, in public. This is direct field support for the load-bearing claim in ①. *Confidence: high, primary source.*

### 1.3 Per-system answers to "does the human hold anything / what happens on total device loss"

| System | Human holds | Total device loss |
|---|---|---|
| **FIDO2 device-bound** | nothing | RP-defined; universally an out-of-band fallback |
| **Synced passkeys** | the platform account password + its recovery | cloud account is the real root |
| **Keybase** | a paper key (optional) | account *reset* — new sigchain lineage, history gone |
| **Matrix/Element** | a 48-char recovery key or passphrase | "reset your digital identity"; prior messages undecryptable |
| **Signal** | a PIN (SVR) | PIN loss is unrecoverable; history restore is still a *future* feature as of Aug 2026 |
| **SSB** | a per-device keypair | *"generate a new identity and tell people to use their new one instead"* — no recovery, by design |
| **Nostr** | one `nsec`, forever | unrecoverable; no rotation, no revocation |
| **AT Proto / Bluesky** | almost always **nothing** — the PDS holds the keys | PDS-mediated; did:plc rotation keys |
| **iCloud Keychain** | the iCloud Security Code | HSM escrow, **10 attempts then destroyed forever** |

---

## 2. THE CONVERGENT SHAPE: four unrelated teams built "person = a group of device keys"

This is the strongest positive result. Four independent teams arrived at structurally the same answer:

**① Ink & Switch, Keyhive (2024–2026)** — the closest live prior art, and it states your model almost verbatim (inkandswitch.com/keyhive/notebook/):
> *"An individual is identified by a single Ed25519 public key — which is immutable — whilst a group is a collection of other principals."* … they model *"a person (or more specifically their authority) as a group, with each of the persons devices being an individual member of the group."*
> *"Key rotation can then be handled by adding a new individual to the group and removing the old one."*

Revocation is group-membership CRDT merge; post-compromise security via BeeKEM. **Note the vocabulary: "their authority", not "them".** *Confidence: high.*

**② Apple iCloud Keychain's syncing circle** — genuinely dyad-recognizes-dyad, deployed to a billion devices. Each device generates its **own** P-384 syncing keypair (not a copied key). A new device joins via **sponsor / applicant / voucher**: an existing device signs a voucher attesting the applicant, adding its public key to the circle. That is "the remaining relations recognize the new dyad," not "prove you know the master secret." *Confidence: high, Apple Platform Security Guide.*

**③ SSB "Fusion Identities"** — *"each device still uses its own identity"* but devices *"share fusion identities with each other."* Multiple device-bound identities federated under a soft aggregate. **Never shipped before the project lost momentum.**

**④ UCAN spec v1.0.0** — states the principle at SHOULD/RECOMMENDED level:
> *"Private keys themselves SHOULD NOT move from one context to another. Keeping keys unique to each physical device and unique per use case is RECOMMENDED to reduce opportunity for keys to leak, and limit blast radius in the case of compromises."*

Authority travels by delegation chain — "sharing authority without sharing keys."

Four unrelated teams converging is evidence the shape is real, not idiosyncratic.

---

## 3. THE LAW OF THE LAST ARTIFACT — the most important finding in this sweep

**Every deployed system that markets "no single key" grew exactly one human-held secret, and none of them publish how often it survives.**

- **Keybase** → paper key. If lost: *account reset*. The NCC audit (p.6) describes the cost precisely: reset creates *"a new subchain… where any prior statements are ignored and a new 'eldest' signing key is declared."* Device list, followers, and third-party proofs all severed.
- **Matrix/Element** → 48-character recovery key. Element's own live help page: *"What happens if I've lost my recovery key? You will need to reset your digital identity, thus your previous messages can not be decrypted and other users will see that you have reset your digital identity."*
- **Signal** → the PIN. signal.org/blog/signal-pins/ (May 19, 2020): *"your PIN isn't recoverable if you forget it."* SVR (Dec 19, 2019) hardens a low-entropy human secret with **SGX + Raft-enforced guess limits (5 guesses)** rather than with entropy. Matthew Green's July 10, 2020 critique names the dark pattern and his own response to it: *"to make it go away, I chose the simplest PIN that the app would allow me to, which was 9512."*
- **Apple** → iCloud Security Code, HSM cluster, *"upon reaching the 10th failure, the HSM cluster destroys the escrow record and the keychain is lost forever."*
- **SSB / Nostr** → no last artifact, and they pay the alternative price openly: identity discontinuity as a real, non-hypothetical cost.

**Three convergent structures, all the same shape:** the marketing says no single key; the practice holds one human-custodied artifact, protected by *rate-limiting rather than by entropy*; and if that artifact is also lost, the system does not recover identity — it lets the user **re-mint a fresh root** and asks the social graph to re-verify out of band.

**Nobody publishes retention or recovery-success telemetry.** We searched Keybase's docs, blog, the NCC audit, Element/Matrix.org, HN, GitHub, OpenAlex and Crossref. *No published figure exists* for what fraction of Keybase users kept a paper key, or what fraction of Matrix users complete device verification. This is an **industry-wide measurement gap**, not a search failure. *Confidence: high on the absence.*

---

## 4. Keybase, measured (the closest large deployment)

- **Model, confirmed:** per-device NaCl keys; a new device is provisioned by an existing device signing a statement about the new key, countersigned. All actions append signed chain links to a public **sigchain**. Merkle root periodically written to a public blockchain to defeat server rollback (originally Bitcoin; one 2026 HN comment claims migration to Stellar — *single-source, low confidence*).
- **NCC Group audit** (keybase.io/docs-assets/blog/NCC_Group_Keybase_KB2018_Public_Report_2019-02-27_v1.3.pdf): Sept 10–28 2018, retested Feb 2019, protocol v2.7.0, 9 person-weeks, 3 consultants. **0 Critical, 1 High, 3 Medium, 1 Low.** Key structural critique: a malicious server *can truncate* a sigchain and present the truncation as true state — caught only by out-of-band social verification or continuous client monitoring, and NCC flags *"it is unclear who has responsibility"* for third-party auditing of the public record. **Only one public audit appears to exist.**
- **Admitted gap:** Keybase dev `oconnor663` on HN — *"there's no way for adding a new device from a paper key to transfer chat history."* The paper key restores **authority, not memory**. This maps exactly onto your model's claim: an edge is re-established; the content that rode the old edges is not.
- **The one moment that matters is racy:** HN user BjoernKW (2017-05-12) reported the paper-key confirmation flow silently advanced past the confirmation screen while he switched to a password manager to save it.
- **Reset friction is deliberate and expensive:** `oconnor663` explains resets are made consequential because *"adversaries in the middle can pretend to be me and act like I've reset my account."* HN user atdrummond (2022-12-26): **locked out 5 days** after initiating a reset.
- **Support load is visible in the issue tracker, unresolved across a decade:** "I lost my paper keys 'WAYNE phone', 'cave cruise' and 'want female'" (keybase/client#21111, filed 2019-11-19, **still open**); "Lost Credentials Loop" (#11304, 2018); "Error Code 218 – Prompt attempts exhausted" (#10389, 2018); "Cannot chat after reset" (#26237, 2024); "I am not receiving account reset information" (#28338, 2025-04-05).
- **Correction to a common assumption:** keybase.io has **not** been shut down. Fetched in 2026, the homepage is still present-tense, no shutdown banner, Tor onion still advertised. The accurate characterization is **indefinite maintenance-mode stagnation since the May 7, 2020 Zoom acquisition** — backend never open-sourced, encrypted Git quietly gone, keybase.pub shut down **March 1, 2023**, and Chia Network still running 20,000+ member chat on it as of 2022.

---

## 5. Matrix cross-signing, measured

- Three key tiers (master / self-signing / user-signing), secrets protected by **SSSS**, unlockable by a 48-char recovery key **or** a human passphrase. *So yes: Matrix reintroduces a human-held secret, explicitly and by necessity.*
- **"Unable to decrypt" is chronic and current.** `gh api search/issues` for `"unable to decrypt"` across `element-hq/element-web`, `element-hq/element-android`, `matrix-org/matrix-rust-sdk` returns **322 matching issues/PRs**, 283 in element-web alone, spanning 2016 → 2026 — including "Unable to decrypt all message history after verifying session with recovery key" (**Aug 26, 2026, open**).
- One HN commenter (Nov 2025) claimed *~10% of users saw at least one UTD message daily*. **Single forum estimate, not telemetry — low-medium confidence.** In the same thread Matrix lead Arathorn acknowledged *"a huge amount of work fixing E2EE issues"* and said that on matrix-rust-sdk-crypto UTD bugs *"should be unheard of."*
- **Why the Rust rewrite happened:** to collapse four parallel E2EE implementations into one core, specifically to stop fixing the same bug class four times. libolm's timing-attack exposure was secondary. Most of 2023 was spent on E2EE bugfixing; the fixes shipped Sept–Oct 2024.
- **Nine enumerated causes of UTD** (blog.neko.dev): no backup restore; all devices logged out; sender signed out; verified-only key sharing; one-time-key exhaustion; Olm ratchet desync; device-list desync; federation latency; device identity key reuse. Note how many are *relational* failures — the edge exists but the recognition didn't propagate.
- The persistent verification failure, from a 2025 "Giving up on Element and Matrix.org" post: *"device cross-verification is another thing that has been haunting Matrix/Element users for years… verifications would either not start at all or simply break in the middle of the process."*
- **NEGATIVE RESULT:** no published verification-completion rate exists anywhere. OpenAlex and Crossref return **no academic papers** studying Matrix cross-signing UX. Genuinely under-studied.

---

## 6. THE DYAD ITSELF — the load-bearing negative result

**Nobody has built or deployed "identity as a signed dyadic edge between a person and a device" as a named, general-purpose primitive.**

The *shape* of the idea has decades of prior art, but every instance frames the edge as **person↔person** or **process↔process**, never **person↔device**. The device is the substrate carrying the key, not a party to the relation.

- **Petname systems** (Stiegler 2005, skyhunter.com/marcs/petnames/IntroPetNames.html) are the deepest theoretical support: keys (global, secure, unmemorable) / nicknames (global, memorable, insecure) / **petnames** (secure, memorable, **private** — "for a particular person, for a particular application, there is a one-to-one mapping between a key and a petname"). A name is not a property of the named thing; it is a fact about a relation. The 2024 **Spritely** revival (Lemmer-Webber, Miller, Larson, Sills, Yaacoby, files.spritely.institute/papers/petnames.html) adds **edge names** — paths through trusted intermediaries, `Alyssa⇒Ben Bitdiddle`. Conceptual; no shipped implementation found.
- **Zooko's Triangle** — the petname answer doesn't square it, it *refuses* it: give up global human-meaningful names, keep meaning local to a relation. That is exactly your model's move.
- **Kim Cameron's Law 4, "Directed Identity"** (2005) — omnidirectional vs unidirectional identifiers. Closest law-level analogue, but frames directionality as a *property an identifier has*, not as the identifier *being* a two-party relation object.
- **did:peer** (identity.foundation/peer-did-method-spec) — still **v1.0 Draft** after years. Repo: **32 stars, 19 forks, 469 commits, 28 open issues.** Real engineering, tiny footprint. **DID Core itself is subject-centric by design** — pairwise-ness is bolted on by a *method*, not baked into the standard.
- **Sovrin — the flagship deployment of pairwise relational identity, and it died.** The **Sovrin Foundation was formally dissolved May 21, 2025**; sovrin.org itself banners *"The Sovrin Foundation Has Been Dissolved — but Sovrin MainNet Remains."* MainNet now runs **read-only on a single cloud server operated by Trinsic, writes permanently disabled** — no new credentials can be issued. Cause per a cited IEEE study: **absence of validator economic incentives**; volunteer stewards stopped paying to run nodes; >$2M accumulated debt. *Medium-high confidence on the dissolution (two sources, one secondary); medium on the causal narrative.* **The model was not refuted — nobody could sustain the infrastructure it needed.**
- **KERI** (Smith, arXiv 1907.02143): identity as a *key-event history* with pre-rotation and witnessed duplicity detection — subject-centric log, relational only at the verification layer. **arXiv citation count: 2.**
- **Object-capability**: Fuchsia ships real capability routing ("no global file system"; each component gets "its own local namespace") — but at the **process/component** level. Capsicum shipped in FreeBSD mainline. **In no shipped ocap system are the endpoints of a capability edge "a person" and "a device."**
- **Local-first (Kleppmann et al. 2019)** names the gap where your dyad would live, verbatim: *"access permissions for documents beyond secret URLs remain an open research question."*
- **Literal term search, live queries:** PubMed for `"human-device dyad"` → **zero results**. `"person-device dyad"` → **zero results**. The brief's assumption that HCI/health-sensing has taken the term does **not** hold under that index. (PubMed-specific; ACM DL and IEEE Xplore NOT REACHED.)

### The two arguments the field would make against the dyad

Both are inferred from verified material rather than quoted from a paper arguing against your exact model:

1. **Devices are too plural and ephemeral to anchor identity.** The industry's actual answer to multi-device is *"N independent per-device keys reconciled by a group-membership protocol"* — DCGKA (Weidner, Kleppmann, Hugenroth, Beresford, ePrint 2020/1281) treats **devices as independent group members**, not as edges bound to a person-node. Keyhive does the same: an individual **is** a public key; the person is the *group*. **Note: this is not a refutation of your model — it is a restatement of it with the person-node removed as unnecessary.** Whether the person-node earns its place is the open question.
2. **The right relational unit may be (person, relationship-context), not (person, device).** Pairwise DIDs, petnames, and Cameron's Law 4 all converge on the edge being between **two parties in a relationship**, with the device as plumbing where that edge's key happens to live.

---

## 7. THE SCALE LADDER — measured, per rung, and the rungs are not alike

**Each rung fails in a categorically different way. This is the most important structural finding for ②.**

### Local / household — the adversary and the trusted party are the same person
- **Matthews et al., CHI 2016, "She'll just grab any device that's closer"** (DOI 10.1145/2858036.2858051, 103 citations), abstract verbatim: *"Many technologies assume a single user will use an account or device… device and account sharing was common, and **mobile phones were often shared despite being considered 'personal' devices**."* Six-type sharing taxonomy; trust in sharees and convenience dominate. **This directly threatens the dyad's premise that a device maps to one person.**
- The **ipvtechresearch.org** corpus (Cornell CETA), confirmed live: Freed et al. *"A Stalker's Paradise"* CHI 2018 (Best Paper); Freed et al. CSCW 2019 (Best Paper HM); Havron et al. USENIX Sec 2019 (founds "clinical computer security"); Chatterjee et al. IEEE S&P 2018; Tseng et al. USENIX Sec 2020 (Distinguished); Roundy et al. IEEE S&P 2020; Bellini et al. CHI 2023 (Best Paper) / CHI 2024 / USENIX Sec 2023 / IEEE S&P 2024 (SoK).
- **Two papers land directly on your model:** Daffalla et al., **"Account Security Interfaces: Important, Unintuitive, and Untrustworthy," USENIX Security 2023 (Distinguished Paper)** — measures how *account-recovery UX itself* becomes the attack surface for a co-resident abuser; and Daffalla et al., **"A Framework for Abusability Analysis: The Case of Passkeys in Interpersonal Threat Models," USENIX Security 2025.** *(Titles/venues/awards confirmed live; the numeric findings inside were NOT REACHED.)*
- Also found: Bicakci, Sekmen, Uzunay, **"Balancing Security and Usability in Enterprise FIDO Passkey Recovery With Peer Assistance," IEEE Access 2025** (DOI 10.1109/access.2025.3602362) — peer-assisted recovery, structurally close to "remaining relations recognize the new dyad." Abstract NOT REACHED.

**Verdict on the household rung: it is the hardest, and the literature says why.** It is the only rung where the adversary and the trusted party are the same person, where "clinical computer security" had to be invented as a discipline because no ordinary security model anticipates the adversary living in the house.

### Community (~100) — failures are legal and political, not identity-technical
- **Freifunk**: ~400 local communities, ~41,000 APs in Germany, coordinated by the Picopeering Agreement with **no central membership register**. The dominant *measured* failure was **German Störerhaftung** (operator secondary liability), which suppressed deployment for years until 2016–2018 ECJ/BGH rulings; the workaround was VPN-tunnelling all traffic through Sweden.
- **NYC Mesh**: >2,000 active member nodes, nonprofit 2019, 501(c)(3) 2020, and a **2026 grant of $898,035 to hire its first paid staff** — implying pure volunteer labour until then.
- **Decidim**: 311 instances across 19 countries (2022); Barcelona 2017 pilot ~40,000 participants, ~11,000 proposals, ~8,000 accepted; three-tier visitor/registered/verified model. Measured failures are political, not technical: Lucerne's top-down deployment *"strengthened the digital divide rather than overcoming it"*; Badalona's participation **stopped when the sponsoring administration lost an election**. Civic identity infrastructure measured as *politically* non-durable.

### Region — failures are root-compromise and statistical exclusion
- **Estonia ROCA (2017)**: ~**750,000** cards issued Oct 2014–Oct 2017 vulnerable to private-key inference from the public key. Certificates suspended **Nov 2–3, 2017**; renewal window to **March 31, 2018**; unrenewed certs revoked **April 1, 2018**. Gemalto paid **€2.2M** (Feb 2021) for failure to disclose in time. **A single-root mass-revocation event with a measured ~5-month wall-clock remediation.**
- **Aadhaar**: ~**1.383B** enrolled (Oct 2024), >99.9% of adults. **UIDAI's own 2018 figure: 12% biometric authentication error rate.** AP/Telangana's 2015 Aadhaar–voter-ID linkage disenfranchised **~3 million voters** before the Supreme Court cancelled it. The Sept 2018 judgment upheld the Act but barred mandatory use for bank accounts, SIMs, and school admissions. This is the anti-model: one root, one number, one human.
- **X-Road Estonia–Finland federation**, connected **Feb 7, 2018**: architecture is peer-to-peer, each organization holds its own signing/auth keypairs, **no central intermediary brokers trust**. A genuinely federated two-region model. **No measured friction data exists** — a real negative result: the cross-region seam is *under-measured relative to every other rung*.

### Cross-region — and where the sovereign root collapsed into two US vendors
- **eIDAS Article 45 open letter**, Nov 2, 2023: **>500 cybersecurity researchers**, plus Internet Society, EDRi, EFF, epicenter.works, Linux Foundation, Mullvad, Mozilla. The objection: any member state can designate keys browsers must trust and **which cannot be revoked without government permission**, enabling *"the government of any EU member state to issue website certificates for interception and surveillance which can be used against every EU citizen, even those not resident in or connected to the issuing member state,"* with *"no independent check or balance."* **Cross-region trust without a common root, resolved by mandating a root — and the field revolted.**
- **The sharpest anti-pattern found anywhere in this sweep**: the German EUDI wallet implementation requires **Google Play Integrity** (Android) or **Apple App Attest** (iOS) for its Mobile Device Verification Method. The implementer's own defence: *"We have to use some kind of attestation mechanism per the eIDAS implementing acts."* GrapheneOS users cannot satisfy it; no mandatory fallback existed initially.
  - GitHub issue **"Do not add Google Play Integrity integration"** (`eu-digital-identity-wallet/av-app-android-wallet-ui#10`, opened **2025-07-16**): **3,042 reactions, 179 comments, closed.**
  - The commenters' objection is precisely a *dyad* objection: *"If someone lands on a sanctions list, they close your account without further notice"* — the attesting party is not the region, it is a foreign vendor, and the vendor can unilaterally sever the dyad.
  - Developers noted a real distinction worth keeping: **smartcards attest identity; attestation attests device integrity.** Different problems.

---

## 8. MULTI-DEVICE REALITY — the numbers that press hardest on ①

- **Cisco AIR**: global networked devices per capita **2.4 (2018) → 3.6 (2023)**. 2023 regional: North America **13.4**, W. Europe 9.4, CEE 4.0, Asia-Pacific 3.1, LatAm 3.1, MEA **1.5**.
- **The distribution matters far more than the mean. Pew Research, US smartphone-only (no home broadband) by household income:**

| Income | 2013 | 2015 | 2018 | 2021 | **2025** |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| <$30k | 12% | 20% | 31% | 27% | **34%** |
| $30–70k | 9% | 15% | 22% | 19% | 19% |
| $70–100k | 5% | 10% | 14% | 6% | 10% |
| $100k+ | 5% | 6% | 9% | 6% | **4%** |

  **Over a third of low-income US households in 2025 hold one device, and that bracket is getting worse (27%→34%) while the top bracket trends to more redundancy (→4%). The gap is widening.** A per-device credential model degrades exactly for the population least able to absorb a lost second edge. *Confidence: high.*
- **ITU 2024**: 5.5B online (68%), 2.6B offline. Mobile ownership age 10+: high-income countries **95%+**, low-income countries **56%** — so **~44% of people in low-income countries own no phone at all.** The floor beneath "one device" is "zero devices, borrowed access." Urban 83% vs rural 48% online. 5G coverage: high-income 84%, low-income **4%**.
- **GSMA 2016**: of 750M+ farmers across 69 countries, ~295M had a phone; only **13M had both a phone and a mobile-money account.**
- **London phone theft** (Met data via ONS): **91,481 (2019) → 117,211 (2024)**, ~28% rise, **~321 phones/day**. One 2025 case traced a gang that shipped **40,000 snatched phones to China**.
- **Kiosk/shared/borrowed**: **RFC 8628 Section 5.4 names phishing as a foreseeable risk in the spec itself.** Measured exploitation: **Storm-2372** (Microsoft Security Blog; active Aug 2024, reported Feb 13 2025, escalated Feb 14 2025) — Russia-aligned; impersonates a known contact on WhatsApp/Signal/Teams, sends a fake meeting invite with an attacker-generated device code, victim enters it **on the legitimate Microsoft login page** and hands over a valid token; then abuses Microsoft's own Authentication Broker client ID to **register rogue devices into Entra ID and mint Primary Refresh Tokens.** No victim count disclosed. *A rogue device registering itself as a dyad is the deployed attack.*
- **Headless / TOFU**: SSH host keys remain the oldest deployed answer, with the documented failure being **warning fatigue** — admins habituate past the mismatch prompt, and it does not scale to many endpoints.

---

## 9. WHERE A KEYLESS-HUMAN DESIGN QUIETLY GREW A KEY — the catalogue

Ranked by how quietly it happened:

1. **Synced passkeys → the platform account.** The human's Apple/Google/Microsoft account password *and its recovery path* becomes the real root of every credential. William Brown (fy.blackhats.net.au, Apr 2024, "Passkeys: A shattered dream"): *"Passkeys are now seen as a way to capture users and audiences into a platform"*; credentials *"can't be extracted or exported in any capacity."* His Dec 2025 follow-up cites **Dr. Paris Buttfield-Addison's case where an account suspension destroyed all stored passkeys irreversibly** — the provider's moderation decision is a total identity-loss event.
2. **A civic region's root → two US vendors.** The German EUDI wallet, above. 3,042 reactions.
3. **Signal → the PIN.** A design that removed the key grew a 4-digit one, hardened by SGX and a 5-guess Raft limit. Green's SGX critique: extract one attestation key + compromise one Signal admin, and *"force Signal to replicate their database onto a simulated SGX enclave."*
4. **Matrix → the 48-character recovery key.** "Identity as a set of mutually-signing devices" ships with a single string whose loss means resetting your digital identity.
5. **Encryption riding on an auth credential.** Tim Cappalli, Feb 2026 (blog.timcappalli.me/p/passkeys-prf-warning/): *"When you overload a credential used for authentication by also using it for encryption, the 'blast radius' for losing that credential becomes immeasurably larger."* His "Erika" case: user deletes a passkey, unaware it was the encryption root; photos gone. *"We cannot, and should not, expect users to know this."* Credential-manager delete dialogs carry no warning. **Directly relevant: if an edge both authenticates and decrypts, cutting the edge destroys the memory.**
6. **SIM / phone number as the ambient root** — SIM-swap remains the most-documented total-identity-loss vector at consumer scale (T-Mobile employee bribery reporting Apr 2024; Dorsey Sept 2019; Buterin Sept 2023).

### The counter-move the incumbents are making — social recovery, deployed

Worth flagging because it is exactly "re-establish a dyad and have remaining relations recognize it," now shipping at Google scale:

- **Google Recovery Contacts** (Oct 16, 2025, The Register): up to **10 trusted contacts** per account; a locked-out user shares a numeric code; the contact number-matches it against three options; **15-minute expiry**; each person may be a recovery contact for up to **25 accounts**; unavailable for Workspace and child accounts; APP participants may help others but not set their own. Google still layers device/location/IP heuristics on top and may still hold the account. Named risk: social-engineering the contact.
- **Vitalik Buterin, "Why we need wide adoption of social recovery wallets"** (Jan 11, 2021): one signing key + **3 or more guardians**, a majority of whom can rotate the signing key; *"for a wallet with 7 guardians to be compromised, 4 of the 7 guardians would need to somehow discover each other and agree to steal the funds"*; **guardians need not be publicly known or know each other.** He does **not** substantially analyse guardian *unavailability*.
- **Apple's sponsor/applicant/voucher circle** (§2.2) is the same primitive, shipped a decade earlier and never named as social recovery.

---

## 10. WHAT RESHAPES THE SHAPE OF THE MODEL (not answers inside it)

Flagged in order of how much they move the geometry:

**A. The person-node may be surplus.** Keyhive, DCGKA, and Signal-style multi-device all reached "N independent per-device principals reconciled by a group-membership protocol" — Keyhive states it as *"a person… as a group, with each of the persons devices being an individual member."* In none of them is the *person* a graph-theoretic peer of the *device*. Your dyad asserts an edge; the field's convergent answer asserts a **set with no centre**. Whether the person-node earns its place — what it carries that the group does not — is the sharpest open question this sweep raises.

**B. A device is not reliably one person's.** CHI 2016 measured phones shared *despite being considered "personal."* Pew measured a third of low-income US households on one device. ITU measured 44% of low-income-country adults owning none. The dyad presumes a partition the world does not honour, and it presumes it *hardest* where the stakes are highest.

**C. The rungs are not a ladder of scale — they are four different problems.** Household fails *relationally* (the adversary lives inside the trust boundary). Community fails *legally and politically* (Störerhaftung; Badalona's election). Region fails at the *root* (750,000 Estonian cards) and by *statistical exclusion* (Aadhaar's 12%). Cross-region fails at *whose root* (Article 45; Play Integrity). A design that treats these as the same problem at four magnifications will be surprised three times.

**D. The cross-region seam is under-measured, not solved.** X-Road is the one verified no-common-root two-region federation, and there is **no published account of what was hard about it.** ICAO PKD (111 participants) operates as a **central broker** — states upload and download rather than exchanging bilaterally — which is a *shared directory*, not a common root, but the CSCA structure and its known gaps were NOT REACHED. If cross-region is first-class in your model, you are building into a measurement vacuum.

**E. "Losing a device is losing one edge" is true of *authority* and false of *memory*.** Keybase: paper-key provisioning cannot transfer chat history. Matrix: identity reset means prior messages stay undecryptable. Cappalli: an auth credential doing double duty as an encryption root turns edge-loss into data-loss. The model's claim survives for standing; it does not automatically survive for what the standing was holding.

**F. The economic failure mode killed the closest deployment, and it was not technical.** Sovrin dissolved May 21, 2025 with >$2M debt and volunteer stewards who stopped paying. *Who runs the infrastructure that lets remaining relations recognize a new dyad, and why do they keep paying?* — that question, not cryptography, ended the flagship.

**G. Attestation is the seam where sovereignty leaks.** A dyad is only as sovereign as whatever vouches that the device is a device. Play Integrity / App Attest is the field's only deployed answer, and it hands the vouching to two foreign corporations who can revoke unilaterally — which is exactly the property the model exists to eliminate.

---

## 11. What I could not reach

**Blocked by the search outage, not by absence:** GSMA Mobile Gender Gap phone-*sharing* rates (India/Bangladesh/Africa); Deloitte per-household device count; smartphone replacement-cycle data (Counterpoint/CIRP/Kantar — and note Wikipedia's uncited "11–18 months" *contradicts* the industry 3–4-year narrative; I take no side); Google's 2019 account-hygiene percentages; Google/Microsoft lockout-after-device-loss rates; the numeric findings inside the Cornell CETA and Daffalla papers; Guifi.net governance critique; Sardex econometrics; MitID complaint data; Swedish BankID fraud figures; EUDI Large-Scale Pilot findings; Reetika Khera / Jean Drèze primary sources; Matter commissioning failure rates and the DAC/PAA hierarchy; FIDO Device Onboard adoption (suspected very low — **unverified, do not treat as established**); Apple Shared iPad mechanism; ChromeOS kiosk identity; caBLE technical/adoption detail; ICAO PKD CSCA structure and gaps; Phil Windley's "relationships not identities" post; Daniel Hardman's peer-DID writing; Mark Miller's *Robust Composition* (erights.org unreachable); did:webs; Bluesky's own migration docs and any did:plc incident history; SSB's decline timeline and stated reasons; Fission's actual fate (fission.codes does not resolve — **circumstantial, not proof**); Nostr nsec-leak incident data; ACM DL and IEEE Xplore full text throughout.

**Genuine negative results, verified as absences:**
- No published figure, anywhere, for Keybase paper-key retention.
- No published Matrix/Element device-verification completion rate; no academic literature on Matrix cross-signing UX.
- No vendor publishes end-to-end recovery-success telemetry for any of these systems.
- No measured friction data for the X-Road two-region federation.
- PubMed: zero results for "human-device dyad" and "person-device dyad."
- **Nobody has deployed identity-as-a-person-device-dyad at community scale — or at any scale.** The one system that deployed the adjacent idea (pairwise relational identity, Sovrin) is dissolved and read-only as of May 2025.

**Single-source / low-confidence, flagged not to be leaned on:** the Keybase Bitcoin→Stellar Merkle-root migration; the "~10% of Matrix users see a daily UTD" figure; the Sovrin causal narrative; Signal PNI terminology (could not confirm via primary source).

---

