A late-completing pass from the scale-ladder spirit landed after I filed. It corrects three numbers and adds one finding that changes a verdict.

---

# ADDENDUM — corrections in place, and one reversal

## Corrections to §7 and §9

**§9 item 2 / §7 cross-region — the open-letter count is exact, and there are two letters, not one.** I reported ">500 cybersecurity researchers." The primary-source PDF (`nce.mpi-sp.org`, redirected from eidas-open-letter.org) states verbatim: **"As of the 26th November 2023, the letter has been signed by 552 scientists and researchers from 42 countries, as well as numerous NGOs."** Signatories include Bart Preneel, Cas Cremers, Steven Murdoch, Jaap-Henk Hoepman, Peter Schwabe. This is **distinct from** the 38-signatory Mozilla/EFF industry letter, which is a separate and later document — I conflated the two.

Two mechanics I under-reported, both load-bearing for cross-region:
- **Article 45a(4)**: a member state's designated keys are revocable **only with that government's permission**.
- **Article 45(2a)** bans browsers from imposing security checks beyond what is expressly permitted — it sets an **upper bound on security rather than a floor**, inverting the normal direction of security regulation. The EFF's historical anchor is **DigiNotar (2011)**, whose CA compromise was used to surveil Iranian users' traffic — the exact failure the article would make harder to prevent.

**§7 Decidim**: 311 instances across **20** countries (2022), not 19.

## A reversal: the Aadhaar evidence is contested, and I gave only one side

I reported Aadhaar as an unambiguous exclusion story. **The field disagrees with itself, and I should have said so.**

- **Muralidharan, Niehaus & Sukhtankar, "Identity Verification Standards in Welfare Programs" (2020)** — large-scale experimental evidence from India's largest social protection program: stricter biometric ID requirements **reduced corruption**, but **1.5–2 million legitimate beneficiaries lost access at some point during the reform**. The authors attribute the adverse effects primarily to **how the transition was managed**, not to biometrics as such.
- **Muralidharan, Niehaus & Sukhtankar, "Building State Capacity: Evidence from Biometric Smartcards in India," American Economic Review 2016** — randomized rollout across **157 subdistricts, 19 million people** in Andhra Pradesh: payments became faster, more predictable and less corrupt, **without adversely affecting program access**, and beneficiaries overwhelmingly preferred the new system.

The disagreement tracks **implementation quality and transition management**, not the underlying concept. For your model this is the sharper reading: the measured harm at region scale sits in the *migration*, not the architecture — which is precisely where "re-establishing a dyad" lives.

## Additions that sharpen §7's household verdict

- **Freed et al., "A Stalker's Paradise," CHI 2018 (Best Paper): 89 participants.** The finding is stated as a thesis, and it is the sharpest articulation of the household problem anywhere in this sweep: IPV attacks are typically **technologically unsophisticated**, carried out by a **"UI-bound adversary"** — an authenticated, adversarial user operating through the normal interface — which "undermine[s] the predominant threat models under which systems have been designed." *At household scale the authorized principal and the adversary are frequently the same authenticated principal.* No amount of credential strength addresses this.
- **Matthews et al., IEEE S&P 2017** frames survivor tech use as a **three-phase lifecycle** — physical control → escape → life apart — each needing different support. Identity-under-coercion is a **state machine with a specific escape transition**, not a static state. Nothing in the corpus treats "leaving the household" as solved.
- **Bellini et al., CHI 2023 (Best Paper): 158 survivors + 16 financial advocates** — four abuser motivations, exploiting shared financial accounts.
- **Cultural variance is measured, and it is large.** "We, three brothers have always known everything of each other" (2021): **59 participants across Bangladesh, Turkey, USA** — sharing behaviour varies by culture, so a single global default (share vs. isolate) is empirically wrong for a large fraction of the world. "Everyone Has Some Personal Stuff" (2019, Bangladesh, 21 participants, 3-week deployment) built a **dual-account shared-password/secret-password model** because single-device-multi-user is the norm there, not the exception. "I would have to evaluate their objections" (2021, **386-person representative US survey**) names a genuinely new identity category: the **incidental user** — someone who did not choose the device but is captured by it.

## One new community-rung finding, and it is a measured negative

**"Detecting and Mitigating Points of Failure in Community Networks," IEEE Trans. Computational Social Systems, 2019** — graph analysis of **ninux.org**, Italy's largest community network. Despite the community's stated goal of a robust self-organized network, the analysis found it **"fragile under several aspects."** The "avoid unnecessary management structures" philosophy shared by ninux, Guifi.net, Freifunk and NYC Mesh **reads as robust until formally analysed**. This is the only directly measured technical-fragility result at community scale in the sweep, and it argues the community rung's self-organization can be structurally brittle in ways its participants cannot see from inside.

## The framing correction that matters most

The scale-ladder spirit's closing judgement, which I endorse and did not state plainly enough in §10D:

**Cross-region is the most argued and least empirically realized rung.** Estonia and Aadhaar are pure *postmortem* — real incidents, real numbers, real remediation clocks. Cross-region is currently **high-quality prediction and expert consensus of concern (552 signatories), not yet high-quality postmortem**. X-Road's actual operational friction is undocumented in open sources; the EUDI Large-Scale Pilot results (POTENTIAL/NOBID/DC4EU/EWC) were unreachable. If cross-region is first-class in your model, you are not building against measured failure — you are building against a warning.

## Gaps this pass confirmed as unresolved rather than absent

Reetika Khera / Jean Drèze primary sources and Jharkhand ration-denial numbers; Swedish BankID elderly-fraud statistics and MitID 2021–22 migration complaints (likely in Swedish Police / Finansiell ID-Teknik and Danish Consumer Council material, outside OpenAlex/HN/Wikipedia coverage); EUDI pilot interim findings (project sites 404/DNS-failed); Guifi.net's "Compact for a Free, Open and Neutral Network" text; Pan-Canadian Trust Framework; ICAO PKD failure data; Sardex quantitative outcomes (papers identified — *Nature Human Behaviour* 2018, LSE 2016, *J. Social Entrepreneurship* 2017 — contents not extracted); Decidim's actual census-verification mechanism for "verified" status.

**Sources added:** [eIDAS open letter, primary PDF](https://nce.mpi-sp.org/) · [EFF — Article 45 will roll back web security by 12 years](https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2023/11/article-45-will-roll-back-web-security-12-years) · [Freed et al., A Stalker's Paradise, CHI 2018](https://doi.org/10.1145/3173574.3174241) · [Bellini et al., Paying the Price, CHI 2023](https://doi.org/10.1145/3544548.3581101) · [Muralidharan, Niehaus & Sukhtankar, AER 2016](https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20141335) · [Detecting and Mitigating Points of Failure in Community Networks, IEEE TCSS 2019](https://doi.org/10.1109/TCSS.2019.2891985) · [ipvtechresearch.org](https://www.ipvtechresearch.org/)
